::PC_U04_L08_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L08_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1; MLA.PC.TR.2
Question:
Which pair correctly matches a triangle method to given information?
{
~A. SSS uses area formula#SSS does not give the included-angle area setup directly.
=B. SAS can use Law of Cosines; two sides with included angle can use area formula#Correct. SAS matches Law of Cosines, and two sides with included angle match A=1/2ab sin(C).
~C. AAS always uses Law of Cosines#AAS is more closely connected to Law of Sines after finding a third angle.
~D. Perimeter alone determines all triangle values#Perimeter alone is not enough information.
}
::PC_U04_L08_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L08_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity is true for all values where both sides are defined?
{
~A. sin(x)+cos(x)=1#Sine and cosine do not always add to 1.
~B. tan(x)+1=sec(x)#The tangent/secant relationship uses squares.
~C. sin^2(x)-cos^2(x)=1#This subtraction statement is not the fundamental identity.
=D. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1#Correct. This is the fundamental Pythagorean identity.
}
::PC_U04_L08_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L08_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula correctly rewrites sin(A+B)?
{
=A. sin(A)cos(B)+cos(A)sin(B)#Correct. This is the sine angle-sum formula.
~B. sin(A)+sin(B)#Sine does not distribute across angle addition.
~C. cos(A)cos(B)-sin(A)sin(B)#This is the cosine sum formula.
~D. 2sin(A)cos(A)#This is the sine double-angle formula, not sine sum.
}
::PC_U04_L08_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L08_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Simplify tan(x)cos(x).
{
~A. cos(x)#This leaves the cosine factor without using the ratio identity.
~B. tan(x)#This repeats tangent instead of simplifying.
=C. sin(x)#Correct. tan(x)=sin(x)/cos(x), so the product simplifies to sin(x).
~D. sec(x)#This uses a reciprocal function, not the ratio identity.
}
::PC_U04_L08_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L08_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
Why should solutions to real-world trigonometric ratio problems be checked?
{
~A. Checking changes the answer#Checking verifies the answer; it does not change valid mathematics.
=B. Context may restrict meaningful values#Correct. Lengths, angles, and contexts can limit which solutions make sense.
~C. All trig equations have no solution#Many trigonometric equations have solutions.
~D. Units are never needed#Units are important in real-world answers.
}